    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Vinnie Pasquantino** commands the top spot in our lineup with a 70.41 efficiency score, driven primarily by a massive 14% platoon edge against Alcantara. He is the clear anchor, benefiting from a soft 4.40 xERA matchup that allows him to maximize his run-production potential in the heart of the order. Following him, **Isaac Paredes** (62.26) and **Brice Turang** (61.40) represent our most reliable assets. **Brice Turang**\u2019s placement in the two-hole provides significant value, and while Gausman is a formidable arm, **Brice Turang**\u2019s dynamic platoon advantage mitigates the risk of a high-strikeout outing. **Isaac Paredes** continues to thrive against right-handed pitching, and the 8% SP skill bump confirms he is our most reliable high-floor play despite facing a pitcher like Castillo.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Cole Young**, who posted a 40.16, falling just shy of the threshold required to displace the struggling **Travis Bazzana** or the low-floor **Max Muncy**. While **Max Muncy** carries a suboptimal 58.59 score due to his bottom-of-the-order placement, his 19% SP skill advantage against Anderson\u2019s 5.25 xERA makes him a high-upside gamble that **Cole Young** simply couldn\u2019t match. Meanwhile, **Seiya Suzuki** remains in the lineup despite a concerning 48.62 score; his matchup against Henderson is the day's primary risk, as the elite arm and negative SP skill adjustment (-19%) threaten to pull his efficiency below the floor. With **Jose Caballero**, **James Wood**, and **Mickey Moniak** sitting out due to team off-days, our bench utility is limited, leaving us heavily reliant on **Josh Naylor** to provide necessary stability in the middle of the order.",
